 also lead to disease  so one of the goals of the Morrison lab  is to develop post-targeted viral  Therapeutics  and so what we envision  and imagine is that there is a balance  between viruses and the host response  and so we look at it as a seesaw and so  what I'm showing you here is an image of  children on a seesaw and they are  representing viruses versus the host  response and in this image we have the  viruses and they're winning so you can  see one red arrow here where the viruses  are clearly winning but if you were to  look at the other red arrow pointing to  that child in the red hat  if we were to just move him over  slightly we could balance that seesaw  and that's what we're aiming to do we  don't want to just dampen the whole  immune response we would like to find  um specific cell types and Pathways that  we could Target that could  allow us to tweak the immune response so  that we can still fight the viruses but  prevent the negative consequences of an  exuberant and overly exuberant immune  response  and so for one of our cases that we're  interested in is influenza and one of  the things that we were trying to do is  to figure out how we can take a host  targeted strategy for treating influenza  infections and to and to do this we went  ahead and compared the host response to  different influenza viruses and so we  asked the question is there a long gene  expression signature that can  distinguish mild and severe influenza  so to do so we use two data sets  um  um one here from  an older data set that I published in  2014 and another similarly designed one  from 2012 from Jos I said Al  and what we have here are mice that have  been bouncy mice that have been infected  with different strains of influenza a  wide range in fact so we have h7n9 Avian  Influenza h7n7  we have h5n1  we have the pandemic H1N1 from 2009  and then in the just sites at all we  have a range of H1N1 seasonal influenzas  the 2009  um H1N1 pandemic strain and we also have  the notorious 1918 reconstructed  um influenza so we have a wide range of  viruses with a wide range of outcomes so  I'm pointing you to this graph here  where on the y-axis we have percent body  weight and on the x-axis we have days  post infection and you can see that  these that the outcomes fall into  roughly three groups so we have here our  seasonal flu viruses that cause on mild  disease the the animals lose a little  bit of weight but then they recover  then we also have some animals that lose  a lot of weight but then do recover  and then we have other animals that lose  so much weight that they have to be  euthanized or they  um die naturally and these animals fall  into our group that are infected with  our Avian Influenza viruses or the 1918  influenza  so we have this wide range of viruses  and then from in both of these studies  we also have viral titers we have a  histopathology data and we have gene  expression data from whole lungs  and the aim for this study was to take  all this data put them together and to  identify tissue signatures and  predictive markers and that's what we  did  so we found that pathogenic viruses  induce a specific Gene signature so let  me walk you through these data  so here I have this large heat map and I  want to point you to the top of the heat  map here where I'm showing you percent  weight loss so I have all of these  viruses here lined up from left to right  from least pathogenic to most pathogenic  based on percent weight loss  so you can see um that line up there and  then  you can see that on the  the left we have different  um  modules  that um different Gene modules and you  can see here let me move forward  that in one module inflammatory red  module we can see that with for the  inflammatory responses that we have more  um expression of inflammatory genes in  with viruses that are more pathogenic so  viruses on the right  we also found that  for genes that are involved in metabolic  responses and tissue repair mechanisms  they are more they are less expressed in  in the lungs of animals that were  infected with the highly pathogenic  viruses and the 1918 strains  what was very interesting to us though  were these modules in the middle these  lipid metabolism coagulation signaling  oxidative stress responses Etc  um that were more highly expressed in  the the  lower pathogenic viruses versus the  higher pathogenic viruses  and so we asked the question what was  actually going on there  what what are the what cell types are  actually driving these differences and  we know that these are long whole lungs  so the lung is made up of a a mixture of  different cell types we have epithelial  cells we have different immune cells  which  lung cells are actually responsible for  these transcriptomic differences  to get a feel for which cells are  actually responsible for this we decided  to take a systems level View and so we  employed this um tissue  um deconvolution algorithm called the  digital cell quantifier or dcq and what  dcq does is to take genome-wide gene  expression data like what we have for  whole lungs  and combines that with an immune cell  compendium from imagen which has a bunch  of  um different immune cell types  and then takes this puts it together so  that it can infer uh  in Vivo Global Dynamic change in the  immune cell quantities within this  complex tissue like the lung  and so in using dcq along with  regression analysis we were able to  identify lung populations that correlate  with weight loss  for example so once again I want you to  take a look at the top of this figure  where we have our weight loss so we  still have our our  um strains here lined up from uh from  left to right we have our least  pathogenic to our most pathogenic  outcomes  and we see that there are various immune  cell types that are correlated with  disease severity for example we have  granulocytes that are shown that are  predicted to be enriched in animals that  have more severe disease outcomes some  more weight loss and this is actually  known in the literature that when you  have more neutrophils in the lung you um  are you have more severe disease  outcomes  we also dcq also predicted that there  would be less alveolar macrophages in  animals that had more severe outcomes  which is also known in the literature  but we weren't interested in using  um dcq as uh validation tool we were  interested in using it as a tool for  discovery  so we were very interested in a  particular population that had popped up  and that we had never seen before  and that population was this small  peritoneal macrophage population  um that we saw here and it was very  curious to us because we asked what are  peritoneal macrophages even doing in the  lung  more importantly what we noticed is that  this population was predicted to be only  in animals that survived infection so  this was very exciting to us here's our  population of macrophages they're  supposed to be in the lung but only in  animals that survive influenza infection  what's going on here  so this is where the hypothesis  generation begins  so we asked ourselves peritoneal  macrophages where are these found well  these are found in the peritoneal cavity  and so what is a peritoneal cavity well  the peritone health cavity is a serosal  cavity and the cirrhosal cavities are  the protein peritoneal cavity which  surrounds our abdominal organs the  pleural cavity which surrounds our lungs  and the pericardial cavity that  surrounds our heart and so these are  fluid-filled cavities that protect and  cushion our organs but also contain  immune cells primarily macrophages and  so because of the relationship between  the pleural cavity and the  peritoneal cavity we hypothesize then  that what we were actually seeing in the  lungs was actually plural macrophages  and not necessarily peritoneal  macrophages  so serosal cavities contain similar cell  populations  um they contain macrophages and serosal  macrophages are divided into two  populations small macrophages  which are MHC Class 2 positive at 480  Negative they are smaller than the large  macrophages and they also are about 10  of the macrophage population  we also have our large several cell  macrophages  and they are MHC plus two negative f4at  positive and they make up about 90 of  the population and they are larger in  size as well  and so we thought perhaps perhaps then  macrophages that we were seeing that  were being predicted to be in the long  in our surviving animals were our small  serosal macrophages and specifically  small plural macrophages so here's our  hypothesis we hypothesize that plural  macrophages were leaving the pleural  cavity  crossing the mesothelial layer and  entering the lung  to do something  perhaps inhibiting influenza replication  or inhibiting damage but doing something  to help promote recovery  um from influenza  so we went ahead and decided to test  this hypothesis and this project was  driven primarily by a student a graduate  student in the lab James Stumpf  and he started off by finding testing  whether or not we could label plural  macrophages in Vivo  and it's notoriously difficult to label  to do intramural in injections but we've  actually worked on a very good technique  and so James was able to intro um to  inject  the um inject the pleural cavity with um  pkh dye which is uh which specifically  labels phagocytic cells  and then he harvested these from the  mice that have been into a purely  injected  and you can see here in red that the  macrophages have picked up the the dye  so yes we are able to label these um  macrophages and we can do it in Vivo  I also want to show you here what  happens when we um  set up an influenza virus infection  um using our model so here is on  intranasal infection with California 9  which is  um  a strain of um influenza that we use  and you can see here  on our y-axis we have um our percent of  initial body weight and on the x-axis 